# Browser acceptance driven by tools/run_project_tests.py --suite browser.
# Most listed drivers bundle the front end, intercept their own HTTP and use
# isolated synthetic media. explicit_creation instead drives
# the real local ASGI app through their bridge with seeded synthetic accounts
# and fixtures, writing only under the runtime directory. None reads a private
# path, personal account or work asset.
!/tests/assembly_edit.browser.mjs
!/tests/explicit_creation.browser.mjs
!/tests/generation_stage.browser.mjs
!/tests/guide_queue.browser.mjs
!/tests/guide_review.browser.mjs
!/tests/media_tools_panel.browser.mjs
!/tests/queued_panels.browser.mjs
!/tests/reference_queue.browser.mjs
!/tests/speech_panel.browser.mjs
# Fixture-driven drivers, deliberately outside --suite browser: they take a completed
# pytest fixture root as argv[2] instead of bundling their own. Their producers --
# test_guide_joins.py and test_creative_inspection.py -- are already published, so
# publishing the drivers completes those pairs. Each reads only the given fixture root;
# guide_joins asks paths.py for the machine runtime root rather than naming one.
!/tests/creative_review.browser.mjs
!/tests/guide_joins.browser.mjs
# The media-backed driver takes a decodable MP4 path rather than a fixture root: its
# producer encodes the file with PyAV into the run root the suite creates. It reads only
# that file and intercepts its own HTTP, so it names no private path or work asset.
!/tests/client_restore.browser.mjs
!/tests/client_restore_media.py
!/tests/test_finish_tasks.py

